===Verb===
{{en-verb||~-ing|pres_ptc2=~ing}}

# {{lb|en|informal|rare|transitive}} To [[address]] (someone) as “[[#Noun|grandma]]”.
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Mary Jane Holmes|Mary J{{quote-gloss|ane}} Holmes]]|chapter=Terrace Hill|title=Bad Hugh; or, The Diamond in the Rough|series=The Chimney Corner Series|seriesvolume=135|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=F{{quote-gloss|rank}} M{{quote-gloss|oore}} Lupton|year=[{{circa|1890}}]|page=15|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/badhughordiamond00holm/page/15/mode/1up|column=1|oclc=22416104|passage=This Laura had died within a year of her marriage, but Lottie had claimed relationship to the family just the same, '''grandma-ing''' Mrs. Richards and aunty-ing the sisters. John, however, was never called uncle, except in fun.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Catherine Kimrey|chapter=14|title=When We All Get to Heaven|location=Washington, D.C.|publisher=[[w:Washington Writers' Publishing House|Washington Writers’ Publishing House]]|year=2003|page=92|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/whenweallgettohe0000kimr/page/92/mode/1up|isbn=0-931846-68-4|passage=“But Grandma—,” I said. {{...}} “And don’t you go ‘'''Grandma-ing'''’ me neither,” she said.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Lauri Robinson|chapter=14|chapterurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=1r2XDwAAQBAJ&pg=PT272|title=A Wife for Big John|location=Adams Basin, N.Y.|publisher=The Wild Rose Press|year=2007|isbn=1-60154-178-3|passage=“Gran-” Nancy started. / “Quit '''Grandma-ing''' me girl.{{nb...}}” Grandma Wilson insisted.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Kristy Acevedo|chapter=21|title=Contribute|location=Mendota Heights, Minn.|publisher=Jolly Fish Press|year=2017|page=238|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/contribute0000acev/page/238/mode/1up|isbn=978-1-63163-098-9|passage=“He can be my doctor anytime.” / “Grandma, eww!” / “Oh, stop '''grandma-ing''' me. You know I hate that. Come introduce us.”}}
# {{lb|en|informal|rare|ambitransitive}} To be or act as a [[grandmother]] to (someone).
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Thomas L. Jackson|chapter=Mission|title=In Any Given Moment|location=[Bloomington, Ind.]|publisher=w:Xlibris|year=2002|page=273|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=L9mdHibMB1gC&pg=PA273|isbn=1-4010-5375-0|passage=And what about Cliff working on cars and painting signs, and Wendy working nights to take care of that baby, and young Allen raking leaves for everyone, and Doris '''grandmaing''' folks, and wounded folks bouncing back to life .&nbsp;.&nbsp;.&nbsp;?”}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Jennifer Brown (author)|Jennifer Brown]]|chapter=The Grill Drill|mainauthor=w:Jack Canfield; w:Mark Victor Hansen; Maria Nickless; Elisa Morgan; Carol McAdoo Rehme|title=Chicken Soup for the Mother of Preschooler’s Soul: Stories to Refresh and Rekindle the Spirit of Moms of Little Ones|series=w:Chicken Soup for the Soul|location=Deerfield Beach, Fla.|publisher=Health Communications, Inc.|year=2006|section=section 8 (Through the Eyes of a Child)|page=185|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/chickensoupformo0000unse_p7o0/page/185/mode/1up|isbn=0-7573-0401-X|passage=“I don’t want you to be a grandma.” / ''But why'', I wondered, worried that he perceived some defect in my '''grandma-ing''' ability. ''Doesn’t he think I’ll be a good grandma?''}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Larry Troxel|chapter=Grandma’ing|title=Poems, Prn|location=Bloomington, Ind.|publisher=w:AuthorHouse|date=15 February 2011|page=19|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=BBkIWIm6aVEC&pg=PA19|isbn=978-1-4567-1501-4|passage=Each is grandma’s favorite child / For them, ever tireless and without guile / They have a special place in her heart / She makes '''grandma’ing''' a work of art.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Timothy Montler|entry=síyaʔ|chapter=Klallam-English Dictionary|title=Klallam Dictionary|location=Seattle, Wash.|publisher=w:University of Washington Press|year=2012|page=381|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/klallamdictionar0000mont/page/381/mode/1up|column=1|isbn=978-0-295-99207-5|passage=2. to act like a grandparent (moving slowly and carefully). {ʔuʔhúy t suʔ''síyaʔ''s. ''She was just '''grandma-ing'''.'' {{small|(MJT)}}&rcub;}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Kelsey Timmerman|chapter=The Starbucks Experience|title=Where Am I Eating? An Adventure Through the Global Food Economy|location=Hoboken, N.J.|publisher=[[w:Wiley (publisher)|John Wiley & Sons, Inc.]]|year=2013|section=part I (Coffee: Product of Colombia)|page=17|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/whereamieatingad0000timm/page/17/mode/1up|isbn=978-1-118-35115-4|passage=Her apron looks like a black belt in '''grandma-ing'''.}}

===Anagrams===
* {{anagrams|en|a=aadgmnr|dragman|grandam}}

{{C|en|Female family members}}
