Page 12 realpolitik: -------- begin text --------
{{also|Realpolitik|réalpolitik}}
==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|Realpolitik}}

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|de|Realpolitik|lit=practical, realistic, fact-based, realism-based politics}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ɹeɪˈɑːlpɒlɪˌtiːk/|a=UK}}
* {{IPA|en|/ɹeɪˈɑlpoʊlɪˌtik/|/ɹiˈɑlpoʊlɪˌtik/|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|iːk|s=5}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|politics}} [[pragmatic|Pragmatic]] but usually [[expansionist]] [[government]] [[policy]] and [[politics]] focused on [[perceive]]d [[national interest]]s of the state to the near exclusion of ethical, moral, and theoretical objectives.
#: {{hyper|en|pragmatism|realism}}
#: {{cot|en|Machiavellianism}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=William of Germany|page=357|author=Stanley Shaw|year=1913|passage=It is '''''Realpolitik''''' in sport, and a Realpolitik which is not wholly unknown in England; but while the spirit of '''''Realpolitik''''' is still perceivable in German sport, it is equally perceivable that the standard English way of viewing sporting competition is becoming more and more approached in Germany.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Liberty and democracy: and other essays in war-time|author=Hartley Burr Alexander|year=1918|passage=Possibly the Germans themselves regard their '''''Realpolitik''''' as but the continuation of the philosophic tradition of disillusionment: like Xenophanes they would remind us that the gods of the Ethiopians are snub-nosed and swart, of the Thracians blue-eyed and red-haired, and that if oxen had gods their gods would be oxen.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=The Riddle of Economics|author=Elisha Ely Garrison|year=1932|passage='''Realpolitik''', in one or another form, is, therefore, from the standpoint of science and of abstract principle, immoral, unsound and no defense of it on the ground of concrete necessity can make it otherwise.|page=254}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=14 August 2013|author=Simon Jenkins|title=Gibraltar and the Falklands deny the logic of history|work=w:The Guardian|url=http://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2013/aug/14/gibraltar-falklands-deny-logic-history|passage=Twenty-first century nation states will no longer tolerate even the mild humiliation of hosting the detritus of 18th- and 19th-century empires. Most European empires were born of the '''realpolitik''' of power, mostly the treaties of Utrecht (1713) and Paris (1763). The same '''realpolitik''' now ordains their dismantling. An early purpose of the United Nations was to bring this about.}}

