#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2013|month=May-June|author=[http://www.americanscientist.org/authors/detail/david-van-tassel David Van Tassel]; [http://www.americanscientist.org/authors/detail/lee-dehaan Lee DeHaan]
|title=[http://www.americanscientist.org/issues/feature/2013/3/wild-plants-to-the-rescue Wild Plants to the Rescue]
|volume=101|issue=3|magazine=w:American Scientist
|passage=Plant breeding is always a numbers game.{{...}}The wild species we use are rich in '''genetic''' variation, and individual plants are highly heterozygous and do not breed true. In addition, we are looking for rare alleles, so the more plants we try, the better.}}
# [[cause|Cause]]d by genes.
# Of or relating to [[origin]] ([[genesis]]). {{defdate|from 1831}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1858|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=NBo1AAAAMAAJ&pg=PA365|title=Year-Book Of Facts In Science And Art For 1858
|passage=All evidence tends to this conclusion, that the sun is the prime '''genetic''' agent of earthquakes and of every other pluto-dynamic impulse which acts against the crust of the planet, and breaks or elevates any of its parts.}}
# {{lb|en|linguistics}} Based on shared membership in a linguistic family.
#: {{ux|en|Chinese has borrowed several words from English, but it does not have a '''genetic''' relationship to English.}}
# {{lb|en|theology}} Based on a shared membership in a religious family.
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Koji Yamashiro|editors=Yochai Ataria; David Gurevitz; Haviva Pedaya; Yuval Neria|title=Trauma and Monotheism: Sugmund Freud’s ''Moses and Monotheism'' and the Possibility of Writing a Traumatic History of Religion|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=IkcWDQAAQBAJ|location=|publisher=Springer International Publishing Switzerland|date=2016-09-15|page=251|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=IkcWDQAAQBAJ&pg=PA251|ISBN=3319294040|DOI=10.1007/978-3-319-29404-9_15|text=Guy Stroumsa (2011) proposes the use of the alternative term Abrahamic religions, emphasizing the '''genetic''' relationship between Judaism, Christianity, and Islam and their branches, for which the idea of monotheism is not always central.}}

