Page 40 cell: -------- begin text --------
==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/sɛl/}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-cell.ogg|a=US}}
* {{homophones|en|cel|sell}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛl|s=1}}

===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*ḱel- (cover)}}
From {{inh|en|enm|celle}}, {{m|enm|selle}}, from {{inh|en|ang|cell}} (attested in inflected forms), from {{der|en|la|cella||chamber, small room, compartment}}, later reinforced by {{der|en|fro|cel}}, {{m|fro|sele}}, {{der|en|fro|cele}}. ultimately from {{der|en|itc-pro|*kelnā}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*ḱelneh₂}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*ḱel-||to cover}}. {{doublet|en|cella|hall}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}
{{wikipedia}}

# A [[single]]-[[room]] [[dwelling]] for a [[hermit]]. {{defdate|from 10th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|passage=So, taking them apart into his '''cell''', / He to that point fit speaches gan to frame […].|6|6}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1764|author=w:Horace Walpole|title=The Castle of Otranto|section=IV
|passage=For three days he and his attendants had wandered in the forest without seeing a human form: but on the evening of the third they came to a '''cell''', in which they found a venerable hermit in the agonies of death.}}
# {{lb|en|now|_|historical}} A small [[monastery]] or [[nunnery]] dependent on a larger religious establishment. {{defdate|from 11th c.}}
# A small [[room]] in a [[monastery]] or [[nunnery]] accommodating one person. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|Gregor Mendel must have spent a good amount of time outside of his '''cell'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2002|author=w:Jennifer Worth|title=[[w:Call the Midwife (book)|Call the Midwife]]|publisher=Phoenix (2012)|page=315|passage=A nunʼs bedroom is properly called a '''cell''' and is small, bare, and plain, without comfort.}}
# A [[room]] in a [[prison]] or [[jail]] for one or more [[inmate]]s. {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
#: {{syn|en|prison cell}}
#: {{ux|en|The combatants spent the night in separate '''cells'''.}}
# Each of the small [[hexagonal]] compartments in a [[honeycomb]]. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
# {{lb|en|biology|now|chiefly|botany}} Any of various [[chamber]]s in a [[tissue]] or [[organism]] having specific functions. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1858|author=w:Asa Gray|title=Introduction to Structural and Systematic Botany|section=fifth edition, p. 282
|passage=Each of the two '''cells''' or lobes of the anther is marked with a lateral line or furrow, running from top to bottom{{nb...}}.}}
# {{lb|en|entomology}} The [[discal]] cell of the [[wing]] of a [[lepidopteran]] [[insect]].
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Specifically, any of the supposed [[compartment]]s of the [[brain]], formerly thought to be the source of specific mental capacities, [[knowledge]], or [[memories]]. {{defdate|14th|19th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Cowper Task|passage=How soft the music of those village bells / {{...}} With easy force it opens all the '''cells''' / Where mem'ry slept.}} <!--book=VI-->
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1794|author=w:Mary Wollstonecraft|title=An Historical and Moral View of the Origin and Progress of the French Revolution
|passage=[W]e shall feel still more contempt for the order of men, who cultivated their faculties, only to enable them to consolidate their power, by leading the ignorant astray; making the learning they concentrated in their '''cells''', a more polished instrument of oppression.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1890|author=w:Oscar Wilde|title=The Picture of Dorian Gray|chapter=XVI|passage=From '''cell''' to '''cell''' of his brain crept the one thought; and the wild desire to live, most terrible of all man's appetites, quickened into force each trembling nerve and fibre.}}
# A [[section]] or [[compartment]] of a larger structure. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete|chiefly|_|literary}} Any small [[dwelling]]; a remote [[nook]], a [[den]]. {{defdate|16th|19th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Montaigne Florio Essayes|chapter=12|book=II|passage=Thou seest but the order and policie of this little '''Cell''' {{transterm|frm|caveau}} wherein thou art placed{{nb...}}.}}
#* {{RQ:Scott Lady of the Lake|II|passage=Not long shall honour'd Douglas dwell, / Like hunted stag, in mountain-'''cell'''{{nb...}}.}}
# A device which stores [[electricity|electrical]] power; used either singly or together in [[battery|batteries]]; the basic unit of a battery. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|This MP3 player runs on 2 AAA '''cells'''.}}
# {{senseid|en|basic unit of a living organism}} {{lb|en|biology}} The basic unit of a living [[organism]], consisting of a quantity of [[protoplasm]] surrounded by a [[cell membrane]], which is able to [[synthesize]] [[protein]]s and [[replicate]] itself. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Paul Brown; Dave King|journal=The Guardian|date=15 Feb 1999
|passage=An American company has applied to experiment in Britain on Parkinson's disease sufferers by injecting their brains with '''cells''' from pigs.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2011|author=Terence Allen; Graham Cowling|title=The Cell: A Very Short Introduction|page=3|publisher=Oxford
|passage=In multicellular organisms, groups of '''cells''' form tissues and tissues come together to form organs.}}
# {{lb|en|meteorology}} A small [[thunderstorm]], caused by [[convection]], that forms ahead of a storm front. {{defdate|from 20th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|There is a powerful storm '''cell''' headed our way.}}
# {{lb|en|cellular automata}} The minimal unit of a [[cellular automaton]] that can change [[state]] and has an associated behavior. {{defdate|from 20th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|The upper right '''cell''' always starts with the color green.}}
#* {{quote-newsgroup|en|date=1988-01-26|author=David Hiebeler|title=Fast way to update grids|newsgroup=comp.theory.cell-automata|url=https://groups.google.com/g/comp.theory.cell-automata/c/3ZCpFpdC3ng/m/y4j-ExTv4EMJ|text=Basically, I'm looking for a fast (the fastest?) way of updating grids, where each '''cell''' has to look at an arbitrary number of its neighbors.}}
#* {{quote-newsgroup|en|date=2010-03-10|author=Jean H.|title=What is CA for?|newsgroup=comp.theory.cell-automata|url=https://groups.google.com/g/comp.theory.cell-automata/c/nxyYlEwjE74/m/ggg6Tq6JqSYJ|text=It is used for land-use change simulations, where the content of the '''cells''' surrounding a central '''cell''' defines how this '''cell''' is going to evolve (for example, a '''cell''' that is "agriculture" but has 3 urban neighbors will likely become urban, while if it is surrounded by forest or other agriculture '''cells''', it will remain agriculture.)}}
#* {{quote-newsgroup|en|date=2022-02-11|author=Mateon1|title=Game of Life with real 8 neighbors|newsgroup=comp.theory.cell-automata|url=https://groups.google.com/g/comp.theory.cell-automata/c/7S3cmItKc9s/m/5g2NidtyBgAJ|text=I've seen this space colloqually referred to as MAP (presumably since it maps a 3x3 neighborhood into a future '''cell''' state)}}
# {{lb|en|card games}} In [[FreeCell]]-type games, a space where one [[card]] can be placed.
# A small [[group]] of people forming part of a larger [[organization]], often an [[outlaw]]ed one. {{defdate|from 20th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|Those three fellows are the local '''cell''' of that organization.}}
#* {{quote-video game|en|developer=w:BioWare|title={{w|Mass Effect (video game)|Mass Effect}}|location=Redwood City|publisher=Electronic Arts|year=2008|system=PC|scene=Salarians: Special Tasks Group Codex entry|isbn=9780784546642|oclc=246633669|passage=Salarian intelligence field agents are grouped into an organization called the Special Tasks Group. STG operators work in independent '''cells''', performing dangerous missions such as counter-terrorism, infiltration, reconnaissance, assassination, and sabotage.}}
# {{lb|en|communication}} A short, fixed-length [[packet]], as in [[Wikipedia:Asynchronous Transfer Mode|asynchronous transfer mode]]. {{defdate|from 20th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|Virtual Channel number 5 received 170 '''cells'''.}}
# {{lb|en|communication}} A region of [[radio]] [[reception]] that is a part of a larger radio [[network]].
#: {{ux|en|I get good reception in my home because it is near a '''cell''' tower.}}
# {{lb|en|geometry}} A [[three-dimensional]] [[facet]] of a [[polytope]]. {{C|en|Higher-dimensional geometry}}
# {{lb|en|statistics}} The unit in a [[statistical]] [[array]] (a [[spreadsheet]], for example) where a [[row]] and a [[column]] [[intersect]].
# {{lb|en|architecture}} The [[space]] [[between]] the [[rib]]s of a [[vault]]ed [[roof]].
# {{lb|en|architecture}} A [[cella]].
# {{lb|en|entomology}} An area of an [[insect]] [[wing]] [[bound]]ed by [[vein]]s.

=====Usage notes=====
In the sense of an electrical device, "cell" is the technically correct name for a single unit of battery-type power storage, whereas a [[battery]] is a device comprising multiple of them, though it is often used for simple cells.

