Page 42 זיך: -------- begin text --------
==Yiddish==

===Etymology===
{{dercat|yi|gmw-pro}}
From {{inh|yi|gmh|sich}}, from {{inh|yi|goh|sih}}, from {{inh|yi|gem-pro|*sik}}, which see for cognates.

Some German dialects use {{m|de|sich}} as a general reflexive of the plural. This usage clearly started out from the {{m+|gmh|-}} accusative variant {{m|gmh|unsich|t=us, ourselves}}, as it is more widespread for the 1st person plural than the 2nd person. However, no dialect seems to allow {{m|de||sich}} for the 1st and 2nd persons singular. At least this part of the Yiddish development was most likely influenced by descendants of {{der|yi|sla-pro|*sę}}, {{m|sla-pro|*sebe}}.

===Pronoun===
{{head|yi|pronoun}}

# {{non-gloss|Reflexive pronoun for all persons and numbers}}: [[itself]], [[himself]], [[herself]], [[oneself]], [[themselves]], [[myself]], [[ourselves]], [[yourself]], [[yourselves]].
# {{lb|yi|Hungarian Yiddish|New York Chassidish}} {{non-gloss|Reflexive pronoun for the third person only}}: [[itself]], [[himself]], [[herself]], [[oneself]], [[themselves]].
-------- end text --------
