====Interjection====
{{en-interj}}

# {{lb|en|colloquial|sometimes|vulgar}} {{non-gloss|Used to express discontent, unhappiness, or anger.}}
#: {{ux|en|Oh, '''hell'''! I got another parking ticket.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Merchant of Venice|II|7|text=O '''hell'''! what have we here?<br>A carrion Death, within whose empty eye<br>There is a written scroll! {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|colloquial|sometimes|vulgar|non-productive}} {{non-gloss|Used to emphasize.}}
#: {{ux|en|'''Hell''', yeah!}}
# {{lb|en|colloquial|sometimes|vulgar}} {{non-gloss|Used to introduce an intensified statement following an understated one}}; nay; not only that, but.
#: {{ux|en|Do it, or, rest assured, there will be no more Middle Eastern crisis – '''hell''', there will be no more Middle East!}}

=====Derived terms=====
* {{l|en|hell's bells}}
* {{l|en|hell no}}
* {{l|en|hells}}
* {{l|en|hell yes}}, {{l|en|hell yeah}}, {{l|en|hells yeah}}
*{{l|en|oh hell}}

=====Translations=====
{{trans-top|hell!}}
* Danish: {{t|da|helvede!}}
* Finnish: {{t+|fi|helvetti!}}
* French: {{t+|fr|merde}}
* German: {{t|de|zur Hölle!}}, {{t+|de|Himmel!|m}}, {{t+|de|Teufel!|m}}
* Hebrew: {{t+|he|לעזאזל!|tr=la'azazél}}
* Icelandic: {{t|is|helvítis!}}
* Japanese: {{t+|ja|畜生|tr=ちくしょう, chikushō}}, {{t+|ja|糞|tr=くそ, kuso}}
* Northern Sami: {{t|se|helvet!}}
* Norwegian: {{t+|no|helvete!}}
* Russian: {{t+|ru|чёрт}}
* Scottish Gaelic: {{t|gd|murt}}
* Spanish: {{t+|es|carajo!}}, {{t+|es|mierda!}}
* Swedish: {{t+|sv|helvete!}}
{{trans-bottom}}

=====See also=====
* {{l|en|damn}}
* {{l|en|heck}}

====Adverb====
{{en-adv|-}}

# {{lb|en|postpositional}} {{alt form|en|the hell|nodot=a}} ''or'' '''{{l|en|like hell}}'''.
#* {{RQ:Fry Liar|page=24|passage=‘{{...|nospace=1}} I know whether a boy is telling me the truth or not.’<br>‘Thank you, sir.’<br>Did he '''hell'''. They never bloody did.}}
# {{lb|en|Australia|NZ|colloquial|sometimes|vulgar}} {{cap|very}}; {{n-g|used to emphasize strongly.}}
#: {{syn|en|hella}}
#: {{ux|en|That was '''hell''' good!}}
#: {{ux|en|They're '''hell''' sexy.}}

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# To make [[hellish]]; to [[place]] (someone) in hell; to make (a place) into a hell. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
# To [[hurry]], [[rush]]. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1929|author=w:William Faulkner|title=The Sound and the Fury
|passage=I had already lost thirteen points, all because she had to come '''helling''' in there at twelve, worrying me about that letter.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To move quickly and loudly; to [[raise hell]] as part of motion.
#: {{ux|en|He was '''helling''' down the road with his radio blaring.}}

===Etymology 2===
From {{der|en|de|hellen||to brighten, burnish}}. Related to {{cog|nl|hel||clear, bright}} and {{cog|de|hell||clear, bright}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|rare|metal-working}} To add [[luster]] to; to [[burnish]] ([[silver]] or [[gold]]).
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1770|author=Godfrey Smith|title=The Laboratory: Or, School of Arts
|passage=To '''hell''' gold or gilt workː take two ounces of tartar, two ounces of sulfur.. and it will give it a fine luster.}}

===Etymology 3===
From {{inh|en|enm|hellen}}, from {{der|en|non|hella|t=to pour}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*halþijaną|t=to incline, tip; to pour out, empty}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*ḱel-|t=to lean, incline}}. Cognate with {{cog|is|hella|t=to pour}}, {{cog|no|helle|t=to pour}}, {{cog|sv|hälla|t=to pour}}. See also {{m+|en|hield}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|rare}} To [[pour]].
#* '''18th century''', [[wikipedia:Josiah Relph|Josiah Relph]], ''The Harvest; or Bashful Shepherd''
#*: Gosh, the sickle went into me handː Down '''hell'd''' the bluid.

