==Hebrew==

===Etymology 1===
Cognate to {{cog|arc|מִן|tr=min}}, {{cog|ar|مِنْ}}.

====Preposition====
{{he-prep|wv=מִן|tr=min}}

# {{alternative form of|he|מ־|מִ־|tr=mi-}}.

=====Usage notes=====
* Usually only used preceding the definite article {{m|he|הַ־|tr=ha-}} or as a standalone form, and in current usage is restricted to formal contexts. Otherwise {{m|he|מִ־|tr=mi-}} is used.

=====Inflection=====
* {{m|he|מִן}} shares its inflected forms with {{m|he|מִ־|tr=mi-}}.

{{he-prep-inflection
|base={{l|he|מִ־|tr=mi-}}, {{l|he|מֵ־|tr=me-}}, {{l|he|מִן|tr=min}}
|1s={{l|he|מִמֶּנִּי|tr=miméni}}
|1p={{l|he|מִמֶּנּוּ|tr=miménu}} <br> {{l|he|מֵאִתָּנוּ|tr=me'itánu}}
|2ms={{l|he|מִמְּךָ|tr=mim'kha}}
|2fs={{l|he|מִמֵּךְ|tr=miméch}}
|2mp={{l|he|מִכֶּם|tr=mikém}} <br> {{l|he|מִמְּכֶם|tr=mimkhém}}<sup>1</sup>
|2fp={{l|he|מִכֶּן|tr=mikén}} <br> {{l|he|מִמְּכֶן|tr=mimkhém}}<sup>1</sup>
|3ms={{l|he|מִמֶּנּוּ|tr=miménu}}<sup>2</sup> <br> {{l|he|הֵימֶּנּוּ|tr=heiménu}}<sup>3</sup>
|3fs={{l|he|מִמֶּנָּה|tr=miména}} <br> {{l|he|הֵימֶּנָּה|tr=heiména}}<sup>3</sup>
|3mp={{l|he|מֵהֶם|tr=meihem}}
|3fp={{l|he|מֵהֶן|tr=meihen}}
|notes=1. The forms {{m|he|ממכם}} and {{m|he|ממכן}} are somewhat informal. <br> 2. The pronunciation ''miméno'' is colloquial.<br />3. The forms {{m|he|הימנו}} and {{m|he|הימנה}} are Mishnaic.
}}

====Further reading====
* {{R:Strong's|H|4480}}

===Etymology 2===
<div>Uncertain. Ebers suggested it was borrowed from {{bor|he|egy|mnw|<hiero>mn:n-nw:W-Hn</hiero>|tr={{l|egy|mnw}}|a type of aromatic plant}}; other scholars have suggested the Egyptian word was instead borrowed from Semitic, or that hypotheses of such a connection are unsubstantiated. The original story in the scriptures mentions that the people said "{{lang|he|מָן הוּא}}" when they first saw it, which the context suggests meant "what is it?". This may have been a {{w|Folk etymology|folk-etymological}} attempt to explain the name.</div>

====Noun====
{{he-noun|pl=-|tr=man|g=m|wv=מָן}}

