Page 134 ey: -------- begin text --------
{{also|-ey|-ey-|EY|ey|əy|eþ}}
==English==

===Etymology 1===
{{inh+|en|enm|ei}}, {{m|enm|ey}}, from {{inh|en|ang|ǣġ}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*aij}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*ajją}}, from {{inh|en|ine-pro|*h₂ōwyóm}}. {{doublet|en|egg|huevo|oeuf|ovum}}.

This native {{m+|en|-}} form was displaced by the {{m+|non|-}}–derived {{m|en|egg}} in the 16th century, most likely due to confusion with the homonym {{m|en|eye}}.

[[File:Eierdoos.JPG|thumb|A box of ten '''eyren'''.]]

====Noun====
{{en-noun|eyren}}

# {{lb|en|obsolete}} An [[egg]]. {{defdate|dated since the 16th century}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1490|author=William Caxton|title=Prologue to ''Eneydos''
|passage=And one of theym... cam in to an hows and axed for mete and specyally he axyd after ''eggys'', and the goode wyf answerde that she could speke no Frenshe. And the marchaunt was angry, for he also coude speke no Frenshe, but wolde have hadde ''egges''; and she understode hym not. And thenne at laste a-nother sayd that he wolde have '''''eyren'''''. Then the good wyf sayd that she understod hym wel. Loo, what sholde a man in thyse dayes now wryte, ''egges'', or '''''eyren'''''? Certaynly it is hard to playse every man, by-cause of dyversite and chaunge of langage.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year='''1787''', originally '''1381'''|title=Liber quotidianus contrarotulatoris garderobae|passage=Take brothe of capons withoute herbes, and breke '''eyren''', and cast into the pot, and make a crudde therof, and colour hit with saffron, and then presse oute the brothe and kerve it on leches; and then take swete creme of almondes, or of cowe mylk, and boyle hit; {{...}}}}

=====Related terms=====
* {{l|en|Cockney}}

===Etymology 2===
{{coin|en|Christine M. Elverson|w=-|in=1975|nobycat=1}} by removing the "th" from {{m|en|they}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{enPR|ā}}, {{IPA|en|/eɪ/}}
* {{homophones|en|a}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪ|s=1}}

====Pronoun====
{{en-pron|desc=third-person singular, nominative case|accusative|em|possessive adjective|eir|possessive noun|eirs|reflexive|emself}}

# {{lb|en|rare|[[Appendix:Glossary#epicene|epicene]]|nonstandard}} {{non-gloss|A [[gender-neutral]] third-person singular subject pronoun, one of the so-called {{w|Spivak pronouns}}, equivalent to the singular {{m|en|they}} and coordinate with gendered pronouns {{m|en|he}} and {{m|en|she}}.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=1975-08-23|title=Ey has a word for it|newspaper=Chicago Tribune|author=Judie Black|section=1|page=12|passage=[[eir|Eir]] sentences would sound smoother since '''ey''' wouldn't clutter them with the old sexist pronouns. And if '''ey''' should trip up in the new usage, '''ey''' would only have [[emself]] to blame.}}
#* {{quote-newsgroup|en|date=1996-12-22|author=Shirley Worth|title=New To Yoga|newsgroup=alt.yoga|id=32BDCA0C.6C8@worth.org|url=http://groups.google.com/group/alt.yoga/msg/4c5da8eb08c0d91b|passage=I'm not familiar with this book, but I encourage Marksmill to look for it-- and while '''ey''' is at it, to also look at a number of other books.}}
#* {{quote-newsgroup|en|date=1997-11-25|author=Scott Robert Dawson|title=Who Pays for Cellular Calls|newsgroup=alt.cellular|id=347acf56.333719@news.interlog.com|url=http://groups.google.com/group/alt.cellular/msg/cffb0aa99cf205e6|passage=If a mobile user is far from [[eir]] home area, '''ey''' will pay a long-distance fee for carriage of the call *from* [[eir]] home area, just as a caller would pay long-distance on a call *to* that area.}}
#* {{seemoreCites|en}}

=====Synonyms=====
* {{l|en|e}}
* ''see'' [[Appendix:English third-person singular pronouns]]

===Etymology 3===
{{word|en|ine|h₂ékʷeh₂}}
{{inh+|en|enm|ei}}, {{m|enm|i}}, {{m|en|ie}}, from {{inh|en|ang|īeġ|ēġ}}, {{m|ang|īġ}}, {{m|ang|īeġ}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*auwju}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*awjō||watery land, floodplain, island}}, earlier {{m|gem-pro||*agwjō}} ~ {{m|gem-pro||*ahwjō|lit=(that which is) of the water}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*h₂ékʷeh₂||flowing water}} {{suf|nocat=1|ine-pro||*-yos|alt2=*-yeh₂}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|UK}} A small [[island]] formed by the [[buildup]] of [[silt]] or [[gravel]] at the [[confluence]] of two [[river]]s or [[stream]]s.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1927| title=Essex Naturalist|author=| page=280| passage=Now it will be seen that each of the three small streamlets named forms, at its junction with the larger river into which it flows, an '''ey''', or island, of this latter kind — Crip's-ey, Dom's-ey, and Pin's-ey, respectively; and I suggest that, from these three '''eys''', each of the three streams indicated derived the final element of its name.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2009| title=War and Rumours of War|author=Julie Wileman | page=81| passage=Runnymede Bridge is situated on an ''''ey'''' – a small gravel islet close to the river bank.}}
# A place that has a name ending in "-ey" because it is or was located at such an island.
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=January 28, 1888|work=Academy and Literature |title=WASA, ISIS, OCK|author=Walter de Gray Birch|volume=33|number=821|page=63|passage=Among the many '''eys''', eyots, or islands, clustering about Oxford, at or near the confluence of the Isis and Cherwell, viz., Binsey, Botley, Hinksey, Iffley, Osney, Oxey, Pixey, &c.,  there are two, vis., Osney and Oxey, which manifestly enshrine this rivername.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1924 | title=Downland Pathways |author=Arthur Hadrian Allcroft | page=76| passage=In Saxon the word ey meant peninsula as well as island, and there are plenty of other '''eys''' about —Langney and Hydeney and Horsey to wit, Chilly and Rickney and Northeye and Mountney.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2018| title=Ghost Trees: Nature and People in a London Parish|author=Bob Gilbert | page=| passage=Bermondsey, Stepney, Hackney; there are many of these ''''eys'''' in London and they were all once islands, or higher, dryer points in the surrounding marshlands. }}

