==Norwegian Bokmål==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|nb|da|mand}} (pre-1907 Riksmål spelling), from {{inh|nb|non|mann}}, [[Appendix:Glossary#accusative|accusative case]] of {{m|non|maðr||man}} (compare the accusative of {{cog|is|maður}}). Originally the word only had the sense "human" but later changed to primarily designate an adult male, the original meaning being replaced by words such as {{m|nb|menneske}} and {{m|nb|person}}. Believed to ultimately be from {{der|nb|gem-pro|*mann-}}, stemming from the {{der|nb|ine-pro|*man-|pos=a root}}. Cognate with {{cog|sv|man}}, {{cog|da|mand}}, {{cog|fo|-}} and {{cog|is|maður}}, {{cog|en|man}} and many others.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|nb|/mɑnː/}}
* {{homophones|nb|man}}
* {{rhymes|nb|ɑnː}}

===Noun===
{{nb-noun-comireg}}

# [[man]] {{gl|an adult male human being}}
#: {{ux|nb|Det sitter tre '''menn''' og to kvinner i styret.|inline=1|There are three men and two women on the board.}}
# {{q|mostly in regular sayings and idioms}} A [[human|human being]], [[person]]
#: {{ux|nb|Den vanlige '''mann'''|inline=1|The man in the street, the ordinary citizen}}
#: {{ux|nb|Gå ned med '''mann''' og mus|inline=1|Be lost with all hands|lit=Go down with man and mouse}}
# A person with certain praiseworthy qualities, often used about males
#: {{ux|nb|Være '''mann''' nok for|inline=1|Be a man enough for}}
#: {{ux|nb|Være en '''mann'''|inline=1|Be a man}}
# One's [[husband]] (''see also {{l|nb|ektemann}}'')
#: {{ux|nb|Hun mistet '''mannen''' sin i en ulykke for tre år siden.|inline=1|She lost her husband in an accident three years ago.}}

====Alternative forms====
* {{alt|nb|mand}}

