==Old English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|ang|ᛗ|man|monn|mon|manna}}

===Etymology===
{{dercat|ang|gmw-pro|inh=1}}
From {{inh|ang|gmw-pro|*mann}}, from {{inh|ang|gem-pro|*mann-}}. Cognate with {{cog|ofs|mon}}, {{cog|osx|mann}}, {{cog|odt|man}}, {{cog|goh|man}}, {{cog|non|maðr}}, {{cog|got|𐌼𐌰𐌽𐌽𐌰}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{ang-IPA|mɑnn}}

===Noun===
{{ang-noun|m|menn}}

# [[person]]
#: {{ux|ang|Man biþ '''mann''' þurh ōðre '''menn'''.|One is a '''person''' through other '''people'''.}}
#: {{ux|ang|Wē '''menn''' wǣron on wambum ġesmiðode ealdra steorrena.|We '''humans''' were forged in the bellies of ancient stars.}}
#* '''c. 995''', [[w:Ælfric of Eynsham|Ælfric]], [https://archive.org/stream/grammatik00aelfuoft#page/n45/mode/2up ''Extracts on Grammar in English'']
#*: {{quote|ang|Ǣġðer is '''mann''' ġe wer ġe wīf.|A '''person''' is either male or female.}}
#* '''c. 992''', [[w:Ælfric of Eynsham|Ælfric]], [https://books.google.com/books?id=m2hVAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA206 "Midlent Sunday"]
#*: {{quote|ang|God ġesċōp æt fruman twēġen '''menn''', wer and wīf.|In the beginning, God created two '''human beings''', a man and a woman.}}
#* '''11th century''', [[anonymous]] [[fragment]] of a ''Life of Saint Mildred''
#*: {{quote|ang|Wæs hēo swīðe ġemyndgu þæt wē eall of twām '''mannum''' cōmon.|She always remembered that we all came from two '''people'''.}}
#* '''late 9th century''', [[w:Alfred the Great|King Alfred's]] [https://books.google.com/books?id=8sy8OOmW-ZAC&pg=PA90 translation] of ''{{w|The Consolation of Philosophy}}''
#*: {{quote|ang|Þā cwæþ hē, "Wāst þū hwæt '''mann''' sīe?" Þā cwæþ iċ, "Iċ wāt þæt hit biþ sāwol and līchama."|Then he said, "Do you know what a '''person''' is?" So I said, "I know it's a soul and a body."}}
#* [https://web.archive.org/web/20180629051659/http://faculty.virginia.edu/OldEnglish/aspr/a3.23.html "The Wife's Lament"]
#*: {{quote|ang|Ongunnon þæt þæs '''mannes''' māgas hyċġan þurh dierne ġeþōht þæt hīe tōdǣlden unc.|The '''person's''' relatives began to think of a secret plan to separate us.}}
#* '''c. 990''', ''{{w|Wessex Gospels}}'', [https://archive.org/stream/dahalgangodspelo00thor#page/n65/mode/2up Matthew 24:38-39]
#*: {{quote|ang|On þǣm dagum ǣr þǣm flōde wǣron '''menn''' etende and drincende, and wīfiġende and ġifte sellende, ōþ þone dæġ þe Nōe on þā earċe ēode, and hīe nysson ǣr sē flōd cōm and nam hīe ealle.|In the days before the flood, '''people''' were eating and drinking, marrying and giving in marriage, until the day when Noah entered the ark, and they didn't know until the flood came and took them all.}}
# [[man]] meaning [[mankind]]
#: {{ux|ang|'''Mann''' biþ '''menn''' wulf.|'''Man''' is a wolf to '''man'''.}}
#: {{ux|ang|Þing sind on weorolde þe sē '''mann''' nǣfre witan ne sċolde.|There are things in the world '''man''' was never meant to know.}}
#* '''late 10th century''', [[w:Ælfric of Eynsham|Ælfric]], [https://books.google.com/books?id=QrZUAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA27 translation of Genesis 1:27]
#*: {{quote|ang|God ġesċōp '''mann''' tō his anlīcnesse.|God created '''man''' in his image.}}
#* '''c. 992''', [[w:Ælfric of Eynsham|Ælfric]], [https://books.google.com/books?id=2xobVW92JLcC&pg=PA302 "Sermon on the Lord's Ascension"]
#*: {{quote|ang|Sē '''mann''' hæfþ sum þing ġemǣne mid ealre ġesċeafte.|'''Man''' has something in common with all of creation.}}
# ''(rare or non-literary)'' [[man]] meaning [[adult]] [[male]]
# the rune {{lang|und|sc=Runr|[[ᛗ]]}}, representing the sound {{IPAchar|/m/}}

====Usage notes====
* Unlike in Modern English, this word rarely refers specifically to males. For such cases, {{m|ang|wer}} is far more common.

