==Old Saxon==

===Etymology===
Common to West and North Germanic: cognate with {{cog|ang|catte}}, {{cog|ofs|katte}}, {{cog|dum|katte}}) ({{cog|nl|kat}}), {{cog|goh|kazza}} ({{cog|de|Katze}}), {{cog|non|ketta}} ({{cog|sv|katta}}). The word existed in the Germanic languages in a masculine gender also, represented in Old Saxon by {{m|osx|katto}}. The word appears to be related to {{cog|LL.|cattus}} as well as to similar words in the Slavic and Celtic languages, but the ultimate source is uncertain.

