Page 284 Lucifer: -------- begin text --------
{{also|lucifer|Lúcifer|lucífer}}
==English==
[[File:Lucifer_(the_morning_star)._Engraving_by_G.H._Frezza,_1704,_Wellcome_V0035916.jpg|thumb|G.H. Frezza, ''Lucifer'', 1704]]

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*lewk-|*bʰer-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|Lucifer}}, from {{der|en|la|Lūcifer|pos=from {{af|la|lūx|t1=light|ferō|t2=bear, carry|nocat=1}}}}. Attested in Old English as Lūċifer. Replaced native calque {{m|ang|lēohtberend||lightbearer}} also from the same Latin source. Application of the name to Satan results from what is probably a misinterpretation of Isaiah 14:12 (whence also the corresponding sense of {{m|en|morning star}}).

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈluːsɪfə/|a=RP}}
* {{hyph|en|Lu|ci|fer}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-Lucifer.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{rhymes|en|uːsɪfə(ɹ)|s=3}}

===Proper noun===
{{en-proper noun}}

# {{lb|en|literary}} The [[planet]] [[Venus]] as the [[daystar]] (or [[morning star]]).
#: {{syn|en|Phosphorus}}
#: {{ant|en|Vesper|Hesperus}}
# {{lb|en|biblical}} The King of Babylon who was compared to the planet Venus in first the {{w|Wycliffe}} version then the [[King James Version]] of [[s:Bible_(King_James)/Isaiah#Chapter_14|Isaiah 14:12]]; it is unclear whether this verse refers to a specific king or to a representation of the entire line of kings of Babylon.
# [[Satan]], the [[Devil]].
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:Satan}}

====Derived terms====
{{der2|en|Lucifer hummingbird|Lucifer sheartail|
Luciferic
|Luciferian
|Luciferist
|proud as Lucifer}}

