Page 288 kid: -------- begin text --------
{{also|Appendix:Variations of "kid"}}
==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/kɪd/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-kid.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪd|s=1}}

===Etymology 1===
[[Image:Goat kid444.jpg|thumb|right|A goat kid.]]
From {{inh|en|enm|kide}}, from {{der|en|non|kið||young goat}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*kidją}}, {{m|gem-pro|*kittīną||goatling, kid}}, perhaps from {{der|en|ine-pro|*gʰaydn-}}, {{m|ine-pro|*ǵʰaydn-||goat}} or {{der|en|ine-pro|*gidʰ-||kid, goatling, little goat}}. Compare {{cog|sv|-}} and {{cog|da|kid}}, {{cog|de|Kitz}} and {{m|de|Kitze}}, {{cog|sq|kedh}} and {{m|sq|kec}}.

Sense of {{m|en|child}} since 1590s as [[cant]], since 1840s in informal use.<ref>{{R:Online Etymology Dictionary}}</ref><ref>{{R:Grose Vulgar Tongue|page=124|passage='''{{smallcaps|Kid}}''', a child.|nodot=1}}</ref>

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}}

# A young [[goat]].
#: {{ux|en|He treated the oxen like they didn't exist, but he treated the goat '''kid''' like a puppy.}}
#* {{RQ:Defoe Crusoe|page=249|passage=I went indeed intending to kill a '''Kid''' out of my own Flock, and bring him home and dreſs it. But as I was going, I ſaw a She Goat lying down in the Shade, and two young '''Kids''' ſitting by her, {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1769|author={{w|Firishta}}|tlr={{w|Alexander Dow}}|title=Tales translated from the Persian of Inatulla of Delhi|volume=I|publisher=P. and W. Wilson et al.|location=Dublin|page=iv|text=The lofty mountains roſe faint to the ſight and loſt their foreheads in the diſtant ſkies: the little hills, cloathed in darker green and ſkirted with embroidered vales, diſcovered the ſecret haunts of '''kids''' and bounding roes.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} [[kidskin|Kidskin]].
#: {{syn|en|kid leather}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1912|author=w:Jean Webster|title=[[s:Daddy-Long-Legs/Letter 3|Daddy-Long-Legs: Letter 3]]
|passage=I have three pairs of '''kid''' gloves. I've had '''kid''' mittens before from the Christmas tree, but never real '''kid''' gloves with five fingers.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} The meat of a young goat.
#: {{syn|en|cabrito}}
#* {{RQ:Scott Ivanhoe|I|VII|pages=85–86|pageref=85|text=So saying, he gathered together, and brought to a flame, the decaying brands which lay scattered on the ample hearth; took from the larger board a mess of pottage and seethed '''kid''', placed it upon the small table at which he had himself supped, and without waiting the Jew's thanks, went to the other side of the hall;—{{nb...|nospace=1}}.}}
# A young [[antelope]].
# {{lb|en|informal}} A [[child]] (usually), [[teenager]], or [[young adult]]; a [[juvenile]].
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:child}}
#: {{ux|en|She's a '''kid'''. It's normal for her to have imaginary friends.}}
#* {{RQ:Dickens Oliver Twist|volume=II|page=12|passage=“So you’ve got the '''kid''',” said Sikes, when they had all reached the room: closing the door as he spoke. ¶ “Yes, here he is,” replied Nancy. ¶ “Did he come quiet?” inquired Sikes. ¶ “Like a lamb,” rejoined Nancy.}}
#* {{RQ:Marsh Beetle|chapter=What the Tidings Were|page=269|passage=I said, “I’ll send the first sane soul I meet to keep you company.” As luck would have it, I never met one,—only '''kids''', and a baker, who wouldn’t leave his cart, or take it with him either.}}
#* {{RQ:C. S. Lewis Magician's Nephew|passage="I should never dream of calling a '''kid''' like you a woman," said Digory loftily.}}
#* {{RQ:Allingham China Governess|The Beanspiller|186|‘No,’ said Luke, grinning at her. ‘You’re not dull enough! {{...}} What about the '''kid'''’s clothes? I don’t suppose they were anything to write home about, but didn’t you keep anything? A bootee or a bit of embroidery or anything at all?’}}
#* {{quote-text|en|date=July 5 2007|author=w:Barack Obama|title=s:Remarks of Senator Barack Obama to the National Education Association Annual Meeting
|passage=Our '''kids''' are why all of you are in this room today. Our '''kids''' are why you wake up wondering how you'll make a difference and go to bed thinking about tomorrow's lesson plan. Our '''kids''' are why you walk into that classroom every day even when you're not getting the support, or the pay, or the respect that you deserve - because you believe that every child should have a chance to succeed; that every child can be taught.}}
#* {{quote-song|en|artist={{w|Drake (musician)|Drake}}|title=March 14|album=Scorpion|year=2018|passage=She not my lover like "Billie Jean", but the '''kid''' is mine}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2019|month=October|author=Ian Walmsley|title=Cleaning up|journal=Modern Railways|page=44|text=Network Rail is now the biggest '''kid''' in the playground, so if it doesn't want to play it doesn't have to, and the trees still fall down every time someone gives a low pressure system a name.}}
# {{lb|en|informal}} A person whose [[childhood]] took place in a particular time period or area.
#: {{ux|en|Only '90s '''kids''' will remember this toy.}}
#: {{ux|en|He's been living in Los Angeles for years now, but he's a Florida '''kid'''.}}
# {{lb|en|informal}} One's son or daughter, regardless of age.
#: {{ux|en|He was their youngest '''kid'''.}}
# {{lb|en|in the vocative}} {{ngd|Used as a form of address for a child, teenager or young adult.}}
#: {{ux|en|No, '''kid''', you didn't do anything wrong; they did!}}
#* {{quote-av|en|year=1942|author=w:Julius J. Epstein; w:Philip G. Epstein; {{w|Howard Koch (screenwriter)|Howard Koch}}|title={{w|Casablanca (film)|Casablanca}}|role=Rick|actor=Humphrey Bogart|passage=Here's looking at you, '''kid'''.}}
# {{lb|en|colloquial}} An [[inexperienced]] person or one in a [[junior]] position.
#* '''2007''' June 3, {{w|Eben Moglen}}, speech, ''Freeing the Mind: Free Software and the end of proprietary culture'',
#*: I remember as a '''kid''' lawyer working at IBM in the summer of 1983, when a large insurance company in Hartford, Connecticut, for the first time asked to buy 12000 IBM PCs in a single order.
# {{lb|en|dated}} A [[deception]]; an act of kidding somebody.
# {{lb|en|nautical}} A small wooden [[mess]] [[tub]] in which [[sailor]]s received their food.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1830|author=James Fenimore Cooper|title=The Water-witch, Or, The Skimmer of the Seas
|passage=peaceable, well-disposed chaps as ever eat duff (dough) out of a '''kid'''}}
#* {{RQ:Melville Omoo|chapter=Reception from the Frenchman|page=109|passage=We fasted till night, when one of the boys came along with a couple of "'''kids'''" containing a thin, saffron-coloured fluid, with oily particles floating on top. The young wag told us this was soup: it turned out to be nothing more than oleaginous warm water.}}

